  "tldr": "Informative dropout leaves an unknown residual survival contribution at every prognostic-score value. Monotonicity and Lipschitz restrictions couple those contributions, so differencing pointwise arm bounds is not a joint identified set. The proposal characterizes the exact compact convex CATE-curve set, proves attainability even where dropout capacity is zero, separates every support direction into arm-profile obstacle problems, certifies each grid problem by a path min-cost-flow dual, and gives a uniform Hausdorff discretization bound. Its open inferential kernel asks for one derivative-aware outer-set procedure that remains uniformly honest as contact sets change and that yields simultaneous intervals after arbitrary selection of an eligible genomic leaf.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Wang et al. identify only covariatewise endpoint bounds and report adaptively favorable ADJUVANT leaves. Random-set, shape-restricted, optimized-value, and subgroup-inference papers each supply one ingredient but do not solve joint curve sharpness with estimated dropout obstacles.",
    "niche": "The missing object is a law-sharp function-valued region whose capacity can vanish, whose numerical representation has a checkable certificate, and whose support map is nonsmooth because its feasible correspondence is estimated.",
    "fill": "The completion lemma and obstacle image settle identification; the support and eroded-grid theorems settle computation and approximation; the open critical-cone construction targets uniform whole-set and post-selection coverage at explicit rates."
  },
  "related_work": "Wang et al. (2025, Theorem 5.1, equations (8)--(9), and Appendix D.2) motivate the clinical problem and supply the shape-free conservative endpoints that Theorem 5 recovers and, under an observable score-pair condition, strictly tightens as a joint region. Beresteanu, Molchanov, and Molinari (2011, Theorem 2.1 and its support-dominance proof) and their 2012 selective-observation paper inform the measurable-selection and support-function architecture but do not specialize it to informative censoring or derive the obstacle completion. Kim (2014, Theorem 1 and Appendix A) informs the use of primal attainability and dual certificates, while the new finite problem is a path obstacle transshipment rather than a fixed-marginal transport coupling. Chiang et al. (2021, Theorem 1 and Appendix A.1) inform the translation of continuous shape restrictions to linear inequalities, but their theorem does not control Hausdorff distance between identified sets. Westling et al. (2024, Theorems 1--3) inform cross-fitted uniform survival signals under identifying censoring assumptions. Semenova (2023, Theorem 3.1) informs orthogonal support-boundary estimation for finite-dimensional set-linear models. Firpo, Galvao, and Parker (2023, Theorems 3.2 and 4.1) and Fang and Santos (2019, Sections 2--3) inform directional inference for nonsmooth optimized maps, but hold no result for an estimated obstacle correspondence with changing zero-capacity contacts. Wei et al. (2023, Theorem 1) inform joint inference for prespecified point-identified subgroups, and Andrews, Kitagawa, and McCloskey (2024) inform selected-scalar inference; the projection result instead protects every eligible leaf of a partially identified curve set. Liu et al. (2021) supply the ADJUVANT genomic destination and the small-cell motivation, not an econometric comparator.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The completion uses the convention \\(\\Delta=\\mathbf 1\\{C\\le T\\}\\), so assigning \\(T=C\\) to the non-maximal branch remains recorded as dropout. If a data system codes ties as observed events, exact attainment at zero residual may fail and the sharp object can become a closure; that alternative convention must be audited before application.",
  "honest_scope": "The law-sharpness, support-flow, grid-Hausdorff, comparator-frontier, and projection claims are theorem targets. Uniform calibration through changing contact sets is explicitly open. When the residual shape restrictions are removed and curves are restricted to \\(\\mathbb C_{\\mathcal Z}\\), the construction collapses to the continuous-curve version of Wang et al.'s conservative pointwise endpoint region. The empirical ADJUVANT reanalysis is conditional on access to the trial records, a predeclared score, and at least one leaf satisfying the mass condition; if every honest sign interval contains zero, the substantive conclusion is non-robust rather than positive.",
